content.text:
verdict: findings
checks:
  - {claim: "F1 / summary 1 — SAFe split", question: "Do the SAFe PM and PO pages say PM owns the ART backlog and PO maximizes team-backlog value as part of Product Management?", source_reopened: "https://framework.scaledagile.com/product-management/ ; https://framework.scaledagile.com/product-owner/", result: pass, notes: "Public summary confirms 'manage and prioritize the ART backlog' and 'part of the larger Product Management function'; body login-walled as declared."}
  - {claim: "F1 / summary 1 — SAFe PO 'only team member empowered to accept stories as done'", question: "Does the SAFe PO page contain this sentence?", source_reopened: "https://framework.scaledagile.com/product-owner/", result: unverified, notes: "Phrase absent from the public page; a web search surfaces it only in third-party training pages and a SAFe 4.0 reference-guide listing. Keep, but mark as secondhand rather than 'search snippet of the same page'."}
  - {claim: "F1 / summary 1 — Pragmatic split by facing", question: "Does Pragmatic say PM is 'messenger of the market' and PO 'development-facing... key stakeholder'?", source_reopened: "https://www.pragmaticinstitute.com/resources/articles/product/comprehensive-guide-product-owner-vs-product-manager/", result: pass, notes: "All four quotes verbatim."}
  - {claim: "F1 / summary 1 — Cagan rejects the split", question: "Does Cagan write 'very weak product', 'no clear owner', 'so long as it was one person'?", source_reopened: "https://www.svpg.com/product-manager-vs-product-owner/ ; https://www.svpg.com/product-manager-vs-product-owner-revisited/", result: pass, notes: "All three verbatim."}
  - {claim: "summary 1 — 'the PO alone is really a backlog administrator'", question: "Does Cagan say the PO is 'really a backlog administrator'?", source_reopened: "https://www.svpg.com/behind-every-great-product/", result: fail, notes: "Source says 'In this model, the product manager is really a backlog administrator' (the CSPO conception of the PM); the draft re-attributes the subject to 'the PO alone'. E4 records it correctly."}
  - {claim: "F1 / summary 1 — Scrum Guide one PO, delegation", question: "Does the Scrum Guide say 'one person, not a committee', 'may delegate... remains accountable', 'must respect their decisions'?", source_reopened: "https://scrumguides.org/scrum-guide.html", result: pass, notes: "All verbatim."}
  - {claim: "F1 reconciliation", question: "Is the reconciliation labeled as the report's judgment, not a sourced finding?", source_reopened: "n/a", result: pass, notes: "Labeled 'judgment' in both summary and F1."}
  - {claim: "F2 / summary 2 — Cagan four risks", question: "Does Cagan assign feasibility to the lead engineer and value/viability to the PM, usability to design?", source_reopened: "https://www.svpg.com/four-big-risks/", result: pass, notes: "Verbatim; source also: 'The Product Designer is responsible for the usability risk'."}
  - {claim: "F2 / summary 2 — Cagan Start Here", question: "Do 'valuable and viable', 'problems to solve', 'feature teams deliver output, but product teams deliver outcomes' appear?", source_reopened: "https://www.svpg.com/product-management-start-here/", result: pass, notes: "Verbatim."}
  - {claim: "F2 / summary 2 — Perri secondhand", question: "Do the secondary accounts carry 'in charge of the why', 'own the roadmaps... priority', 'Agile does not have a brain', 'no measures of success'?", source_reopened: "https://www.produxlabs.com/product-thinking-blog/episode-252-project-product-management ; https://www.mindtheproduct.com/escaping-build-trap-melissa-perri/", result: pass, notes: "Verbatim; correctly marked secondhand."}
  - {claim: "F2 / summary 2 — Pragmatic 'tell... about the problem'", question: "Does the Pragmatic article say the PM tells development about the problem?", source_reopened: "https://www.pragmaticinstitute.com/resources/articles/product/role-of-product-management/", result: pass, notes: "Verbatim."}
  - {claim: "F2 — Mind the Product 'no positional power'", question: "Does the announcement contain the sentence?", source_reopened: "https://www.mindtheproduct.com/product-management-growth-team-effort/", result: pass, notes: "Verbatim; correctly marked abstract."}
  - {claim: "F3 / summary 3 — Scrum Guide PO accountabilities, sizing, DoD", question: "Does the Guide list the four backlog accountabilities, give sizing to Developers, and DoD to the Scrum Team?", source_reopened: "https://scrumguides.org/scrum-guide.html", result: pass, notes: "Verbatim; DoD line is a paraphrase of 'the Scrum Team must create a Definition of Done appropriate for the product' — mark as paraphrase."}
  - {claim: "F3 / summary 3 — Cucumber three amigos", question: "Do the Cucumber pages say PO brings scope, tester scenarios, developer steps, pair reviewed by PO, PO identifies the problem, 'weak-ass move'?", source_reopened: "https://cucumber.io/docs/bdd/who-does-what/ ; https://cucumber.io/docs/bdd/discovery-workshop/ ; https://cucumber.io/blog/bdd/who-writes-the-cucumber-scenarios/", result: pass, notes: "All verbatim; discovery-workshop text is 'constraints/acceptance criteria the team has agreed upon' (slash, minor)."}
  - {claim: "F3 — Humanizing Work splitting", question: "Do 'Invite a whole team...' and 'concrete change in system behavior' appear?", source_reopened: "https://www.humanizingwork.com/the-humanizing-work-guide-to-splitting-user-stories/", result: pass, notes: "Verbatim."}
  - {claim: "F4 / summary 4 — Cohn", question: "Does Cohn say 'what to build, not how' and 'sparingly, wisely and ideally, in consultation with the team'?", source_reopened: "https://www.mountaingoatsoftware.com/blog/can-a-product-owner-dictate-the-architecture", result: pass, notes: "Verbatim."}
  - {claim: "F4 / summary 4 — Wolpers 'How' anti-pattern", question: "Does Wolpers say the PO supplying the 'How' is an anti-pattern and Developers answer the 'How'?", source_reopened: "https://age-of-product.com/product-owner-anti-patterns/", result: pass, notes: "Verbatim; draft's ellipsis drops '(Just stick with the Scrum Guide and its built-in checks & balances:' — acceptable but should show the elision."}
  - {claim: "F4 / summary 4 — Xebia", question: "Does Xebia say the PO ultimately chooses design ideas and business-cased ideas go on the backlog?", source_reopened: "https://xebia.com/blog/architects-scrum-4-what-is-the-role-of-the-architect-in-scrum/", result: pass, notes: "Verbatim."}
  - {claim: "F4 — SAFe architect enablers/NFRs", question: "Do the SAFe summaries say the architect defines enablers/NFRs and NFRs are revisited in DoD?", source_reopened: "https://framework.scaledagile.com/system-architect/ ; https://framework.scaledagile.com/nonfunctional-requirements/", result: pass, notes: "Verbatim in public summaries; login-walled body as declared."}
  - {claim: "F4 — agileseekers practitioner quote", question: "Does agileseekers say 'PO/PMs should work closely with architects to ensure that the backlog reflects these requirements accurately and is prioritized appropriately'?", source_reopened: "https://agileseekers.com/blog/collaborating-with-system-architects-to-refine-non-functional-enablers", result: fail, notes: "Source reads 'Product Owners and Managers should work closely with architects to ensure that these items are appropriately tracked and prioritized appropriately in the backlog.' Draft's quote is a paraphrase presented as verbatim."}
  - {claim: "F5 / summary 5 — Torres product trio", question: "Do 'typically comprised of', 'at a minimum', 'jointly responsible for a shared outcome', together-clauses, 'mercenary teams' appear?", source_reopened: "https://www.producttalk.org/product-trio/", result: pass, notes: "Verbatim; note Torres attributes 'mercenary teams' to Cagan."}
  - {claim: "F5 / summary 5 — Illustrated Agile", question: "Does it say the Architect ensures technical feasibility and the PO is ultimately responsible for product decisions?", source_reopened: "https://illustratedagile.com/2012/07/10/powerful-partnership-product-owner-designer-architects/", result: pass, notes: "Verbatim."}
  - {claim: "F5 — decode.agency", question: "Does it say feasibility is the solution architect's most important discovery task?", source_reopened: "https://decode.agency/article/solution-architect-in-product-discovery/", result: pass, notes: "Verbatim."}
  - {claim: "F5 / summary 5 — SAFe co-leaders, Aha! capacity", question: "Does SAFe name PM and architect as ART leaders, and Aha! say PMs allocate capacity with architects?", source_reopened: "https://framework.scaledagile.com/product-management/ ; https://www.aha.io/roadmapping/guide/product-development-methodologies/what-is-the-role-of-pm-in-safe", result: pass, notes: "Verbatim; 'co-leaders' is the draft's gloss of 'as leaders of the Agile Release Train'."}
  - {claim: "F5 — LogRocket sample RACI", question: "Is the PM Accountable at every stage and the architect Responsible only in Design; 'ONE single person'?", source_reopened: "https://blog.logrocket.com/product-management/sample-raci-chart-best-practices-template/", result: pass, notes: "Confirmed."}
  - {claim: "F6 / summary 6 — feature factory, waiter", question: "Do LogRocket and Larson carry the quoted lines?", source_reopened: "https://blog.logrocket.com/product-management/build-trap-dangers-feature-factory-mindset/ ; https://lethain.com/notes-escaping-the-build-trap/", result: pass, notes: "Verbatim; Larson's is 'leadership had passed down feature requests rather than expected outcomes and goals'."}
  - {claim: "F6 / summary 6 — ticket monkey, waterfall 2.0", question: "Does Wolpers carry both?", source_reopened: "https://age-of-product.com/product-owner-anti-patterns/", result: pass, notes: "Verbatim."}
  - {claim: "F6 / summary 6 — dual accountability", question: "Does Meegle say 'power struggles and decision-making delays'?", source_reopened: "https://www.meegle.com/en_us/topics/raci-matrix/raci-matrix-for-product-ownership", result: pass, notes: "Verbatim."}
  - {claim: "F6 / summary 6 — architecture astronaut", question: "Does Wikipedia carry the Spolsky passage?", source_reopened: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Architecture_astronaut", result: pass, notes: "Verbatim."}
  - {claim: "F7 / summary 7 — usability has its own owner", question: "Do Cagan and Torres assign usability/design a seat?", source_reopened: "https://www.svpg.com/four-big-risks/ ; https://www.producttalk.org/product-trio/", result: pass, notes: "Cagan: 'The Product Designer is responsible for the usability risk'; Torres: designer is one of the three minimum roles. Gap is labeled as the report's observation."}
confidence_labels: "all defensible — F4 'medium' is conservative (Cohn primary plus Wolpers and Xebia in full agree; 'high' would also be defensible); F6 medium matches all-secondary sourcing; no label combines confidence with likelihood. Method's page counts (30 full / 5 abstract) could not be checked."
top_changes:
  - "Summary item 1: change 'the PO alone \"is really a backlog administrator\"' to Cagan's actual subject — 'the product manager is really a backlog administrator' under the CSPO conception (source: behind-every-great-product), or drop the quote from item 1."
  - "F4: replace the agileseekers quote with the source's wording — 'Product Owners and Managers should work closely with architects to ensure that these items are appropriately tracked and prioritized appropriately in the backlog.' — or mark it as a paraphrase."
  - "F1: relabel the SAFe 'only team member empowered to accept stories as done' line as secondhand (third-party training/reference pages), not 'a search snippet of the same page'; it does not appear on the public PO page."
  - "F3: mark 'the Definition of Done is the Scrum Team's' as a paraphrase of 'the Scrum Team must create a Definition of Done appropriate for the product' (if not an organizational standard)."
  - "F4 Wolpers: show the elision — 'How' and the 'What.' (Just stick with the Scrum Guide and its built-in checks & balances: The Developers answer the 'How' question...)."
